  "content": {
    "text": "While US stocks (^DJI, ^IXIC, ^GSPC) are mixed as investors continue to digest the Trump administration's reported peace deal with Iran, oil prices (CL=F, BZ=F) have sunk below $80 per barrel but remain elevated above pre-war prices.\nYahoo Finance Senior Reporters Brooke DiPalma and Ines Ferré assess the impact these geopolitical headlines are having on oil and domestic US gas prices (RB=F).\noil has been declining and the expectation is that the Straight of Hormuz, if this deal gets signed, goes through, then the Straight of Hormuz could start reopening, um, by the end of this week.\nand Goldman Sachs is now saying that they are assuming that by the end of July, you would have oil flowing back again, uh, and resuming normally.\nAnd so what this means is that you will see oil coming down even further. You've got Goldman that is now dropping their Q4 forecast for Brent to $80 a barrel. You've got WTI that's also being dropped to $75 a barrel.\nSo, this rally that we've been seeing, uh this SpaceX enthusiasm, the rally that we've been seeing in semis. Remember, the AI trade is back up now. The semiconductor stocks are have been up again to all-time highs, Micron, SanDisk, uh, Western Digital.\nIf you take a look at this entire rally, it's not happening in a vacuum. It is happening with this tailwind of, okay, we may now see that oil comes down meaningfully and the Fed may see this energy shock as temporary, perhaps.\nBrooke, this is a big tailwind for the consumer.\nThis is a big tailwind for the consumer. When you think about just how much money they've been quite literally pumping into their uh, you know, gas tanks over these past three months, that had really weighed on consumer sentiment, which we saw slightly improved on Friday morning, largely it wasn't really a headline because SpaceX uh took over the conversation.\nBut we actually are at this pivotal moment right now where we continue to see inflation surpass wage growth, and that's been something that we've been watching and keeping a close eye now because it's been consecutively happening over the past few months.\nAnd that's sort of eating into low and middle-income consumers according to Bank of America's ability, uh, these low and middle-income consumer's ability to spend, whereas high-income earners continue to be able to spend because their wage growth isn't necessarily, uh, you know, coming in less than what we're seeing on inflation right now.\nAnd so this is something definitely to watch. We want to be able to see the consumer who, when you think about how much the consumer contributes is over half of to the US economy. And so this is really crucial, especially heading into these deal days. We know we have Amazon Prime Day coming up shortly, and also we expect other retailers like Walmart and Target to also have their own sort of deal days.\nSo, this is really pivotal in order for these days for these mega companies to be successful, the consumer needs to feel like they have extra room in their wallet to be able to spend right now.",
